<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#yemsa-language">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yemsa language</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Janjero language</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yemsa, formerly called Janjero, is an Omotic language of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, spoken by the Yem people in the hills east of the Omo river. It was the language of the Kingdom of Janjero, which was annexed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> in 1894. Many Yem now also speak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a>.</p></div>
